# --- s016 Book I d.3 q.1 (= Ord. I d.3 p.1 qq.1–2, nn. 1–103)
n("n331","s016-01","sunt vndecim questiones","la","commentary","Gorris counts eleven questions in d. 3 (the *Ordinatio* has four in p. 1, three in p. 2, four in p. 3). His q. 1 combines Scotus's p. 1 qq. 1–2 (*utrum Deus sit naturaliter cognoscibilis*; *utrum Deus sit primum cognitum*), as the phrase *in prima sunt due* says.","verified")
n("n332","s016-01","Deum nemo vidit vnquam","la","source","John 1:18. *Ad oppositum*: John 14:7 (*et amodo cognoscetis eum et vidistis eum*). A's *Jo. 4* should be *Jo. 14*.","certain")
n("n333","s016-02","sicut distinguit Tho. prima parte summe. q. 12. arti. 12","la","source","*Ord.* I d. 3 p. 1 q. 1–2 nn. 10–12 rejects three preliminary distinctions (negative/affirmative knowledge; *quia est* / *si est*; *si est* as the truth of a proposition). Gorris assigns the first and third to Thomas: *ST* I q. 12 a. 12 (natural knowledge of God through creatures) and *ST* I q. 3 a. 4 ad 2 (*esse* as act of being versus as the composition of a proposition). The second attribution is exact.","verified")
n("n334","s016-02","doctoris solennis in summa articulo. 22. q. 2. et 3","la","source","Henry of Ghent, the *Doctor solemnis*. Scotus discusses his view in *Ord.* I d. 3 nn. 16–25. Henry's natural knowledge of God is treated in *Summa* aa. 22–24. The Vatican apparatus cites a. 24 q. 6 for the core passage, so Gorris's *a. 22 qq. 2–3* may refer to the cognate *utrum Deus sit primum cognitum*.","to-verify")
n("n335","s016-02","vniuocationem","la","source","*Ord.* I d. 3 nn. 26–55: univocity of the concept of being. Gorris's gloss *substantie et accidenti* takes in n. 26's *univocum Deo et creaturae* and the extension in nn. 129ff.","verified")
n("n336","s016-02","q. 12. articulo. 4","la","source","*ST* I q. 12 a. 4: the proper object of the human intellect is the quiddity of material things. Scotus reports this view in d. 3 n. 186ff. (q. 3). B reads *q. xij arti.* followed by a numeral that is damaged at the line break.","certain")
n("n337","s016-03","Prima quod possumus habere aliquem conceptum","la","source","The five *dicta* of *Ord.* I d. 3 nn. 25 (*Primo dico*), 26 (univocity), 56 (*Tertio dico… non in particulari et proprio*), 58 (*Quarto… ad multos conceptus proprios Deo*), 61 (*Quinto… per species creaturarum*). Gorris's fourth conclusion turns Scotus's *proprios* into *non proprii sed communes*. That is a misreading, or perhaps a deliberate simplification, since Scotus's *proprii* are those common to all perfections *simpliciter* as they are in God (*ens infinitum*).","verified")
n("n338","s016-03","quamdiu vixerimus in hoc mundo","la","source","Not in Scotus's text here; *Ord.* I d. 3 n. 56 has only *non cognoscitur in particulari et proprio*. The saying recalls Thomas, *De ente* c. 5 (*differentiae essentiales rerum nobis ignotae sunt*). The ascription to the punishment of sin is Augustinian in flavour. B has the same text.","uncertain")
n("n339","s016-03","Inuisibilia dei per ea que facta sunt","la","source","Rom 1:20.","verified")
n("n340","s016-04","triplex est primitas","la","source","*Ord.* I d. 3 n. 70: *primitas originis*, *perfectionis*, *adaequationis*. Arist. *Metaph.* IX 8, 1050a4 (prior in generation, posterior in substance). Arist. *Anal. post.* I 4, 73b25ff. (*primo inest*).","verified")
n("n341","s016-04","visio Aquile","la","source","*Ord.* I d. 3 n. 95: *visio aquilae de sole est simpliciter perfectior quam visio mea de candela*. Gorris adds the explanation of the 'proportional' sense.","verified")
n("n342","s016-04","triplex est cognitio","la","source","The division of actual, habitual and virtual knowledge corresponds to *Ord.* I d. 3 nn. 71 and 92 (*Quantum ad notitiam habitualem vel virtualem…*).","verified")
n("n343","s016-05","Auic. p°. metha. c. 3","la","source","*Ord.* I d. 3 n. 77: *Avicenna I Metaphysicae cap. 3: metaphysica est ultima in ordine doctrinae*. Gorris's *in ordine confuse concipiendi vel ens* compresses this, and the phrase *vel ens* is obscure. Scotus's point is that what is last in learning is first in the order of nature for distinct knowledge. A and B agree.","uncertain")
n("n344","s016-05","Quarta conclusio quod primum cognitum perfectione simpliciter est deus","la","source","*Ord.* I d. 3 n. 97, with the same appeal to *Eth. Nic.* X 7–8.","verified")
n("n345","s016-06","noticia intuitiua","la","commentary","Scotus's reply to Jn 1:18: no one has seen God by intuitive vision in this life (*Ord.* I d. 3 n. 102 or thereabouts).","to-verify")
n("n346","s016-01","John 4","en","translation","Read John 14:7. A's *4* is probably a misprint for *14*.")
n("n347","s016-02","Solemn Doctor","en","translation","*doctor solennis*: Henry of Ghent's scholastic title.")
n("n348","s016-05","or being [is]","en","translation","The Latin *vel ens* is elliptical. See the corresponding note on the Latin.")
# --- B collation (full): B29L–B30L
upd("n332", append=" B also reads *ioh. iiij.*, so the error is common to both editions.")
n("n1075","s016-02","concedendo cum breuiter","la","textual","B: *concedendo ipsum esse breuiter*. B's *ipsum* supplies the subject that A's *cum* lacks; the translation ('granting in brief that it is') already gives this sense.","certain")
n("n1076","s016-03","per species creaturarum","la","textual","B: *per species creatas*.","certain")
n("n1077","s016-05","Item quia si aliqua","la","textual","B: *Quia si aliqua* (without *Item*).","certain")
